==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|ang|en}} {{term|hæfen|lang=ang}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*habnō}}, {{m|gem-pro|*habanō}} (compare Dutch {{term|haven|lang=nl}}, German {{term|Hafen|lang=de}}, Danish/Norwegian {{term|havn|lang=da}}), from {{attention|gem-pro|is the following word Proto-Germanic, or what?}} {{m|und|haban||sea}} (compare Old English {{term|hæf|lang=ang}}, German {{term|Haff|lang=de}} 'gulf', Danish {{term|hav|lang=da}}), or from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*kopno-}} (compare Irish {{term|cúan|lang=ga}} 'harbor, recess, haven').

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈheɪvən/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|En-us-haven.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|eɪvən|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[harbour]] or [[anchorage]] protected from the sea.
#* Shakespeare
#*: what shipping and what lading's in our '''haven'''
#* Tennyson
#*: their '''haven''' under the hill
# {{context|by extension|lang=en}} A place of [[safety]]; a refuge or sanctuary.
#* {{quote-news
|year=2011
|date=December 21
|author=Helen Pidd
|title=Europeans migrate south as continent drifts deeper into crisis
|work=the Guardian
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/21/europe-migrants-crisis-irish-portuguese
|page=
|passage=Since its conception, the European Union has been a '''haven''' for those seeking refuge from war, persecution and poverty in other parts of the world.}}

